
At some point one may need to create a local certificate : 

    openssl req -x509 -nodes -days 365 \
            -newkey rsa:4096 \
            -keyout my_local_cert.pem \
            -out my_lcoal_cert.pem

--------------------------------------------------------------------

The test file "the_merchant_of_venice.txt" was lifted with tender
thanks to The Project Gutenberg :

    https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/1515/pg1515.txt

--------------------------------------------------------------------

The code is trivial and only expects that you provide a
message digest algorithm and an optional text string.

If you provide nothing then you get told a silly usage
message : 

$ ./dgst_test 
Usage: ./dgst_test digest_algorithm_name

If you do not know of a message digest algorithm or you
spell it wrong then you get told a list of valid algorithms
like so : 

$ ./dgst_test foodoo
FAIL : Unknown message digest foodoo
    : Please use one of the following :
    :   md4, md5, mdc2, rmd160, gost, sha1,
    :   sm3, shake128, shake256, sha224,
    :   sha256, sha384, sha512,
    :   sha512-224, sha512-256, sha3-224,
    :   sha3-256, sha3-384, sha3-512,
    :   blake2s256, blake2b512.
$ 

However different machines and different OpenSSL versions may
report other options : 

oberon$ uname -apKU
FreeBSD oberon 14.0-CURRENT FreeBSD 14.0-CURRENT #1 main-n251923-4bae154fe8c: Mon Dec 27 07:21:52 UTC 2021     root@oberon:/usr/obj/usr/src/arm64.aarch64/sys/GENERIC  arm64 aarch64 1400045 1400045
oberon$ 
oberon$ openssl version
OpenSSL 1.1.1m-freebsd  14 Dec 2021
oberon$ 
oberon$ openssl dgst -list
Supported digests:
-blake2b512                -blake2s256                -md4                      
-md5                       -md5-sha1                  -mdc2                     
-ripemd                    -ripemd160                 -rmd160                   
-sha1                      -sha224                    -sha256                   
-sha3-224                  -sha3-256                  -sha3-384                 
-sha3-512                  -sha384                    -sha512                   
-sha512-224                -sha512-256                -shake128                 
-shake256                  -sm3                       -ssl3-md5                 
-ssl3-sha1                 -whirlpool                 
oberon$ 


You may then select one of those :


$ ./dgst_test blake2b512
Input is 3 bytes : "abc"
    61 62 63 

Message digest algorithm blake2b512 :
    BA 80 A5 3F 98 1C 4D 0D 6A 27 97 B6 9F 12 F6 E9 
    4C 21 2F 14 68 5A C4 B7 4B 12 BB 6F DB FF A2 D1 
    7D 87 C5 39 2A AB 79 2D C2 52 D5 DE 45 33 CC 95 
    18 D3 8A A8 DB F1 92 5A B9 23 86 ED D4 00 99 23 
$ 

Looking at the above you see that the default input message
is the text string "abc" and the output seems to make sense
given the test data in RFC 7693 Appendix A which you may see
at https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7693.txt

Feel free to use some other algorithm :

$ ./dgst_test sha256
Input is 3 bytes : "abc"
    61 62 63 

Message digest algorithm sha256 :
    BA 78 16 BF 8F 01 CF EA 41 41 40 DE 5D AE 22 23 
    B0 03 61 A3 96 17 7A 9C B4 10 FF 61 F2 00 15 AD 
$ 

You may also provide a test string :

$ ./dgst_test sha256 what\ the\ cat\ dragged\ in
Input is 23 bytes : "what the cat dragged in"
    77 68 61 74 20 74 68 65 20 63 61 74 20 64 72 61 
    67 67 65 64 20 69 6E 

Message digest algorithm sha256 :
    BF E8 30 24 F3 C3 AC 10 F5 C5 53 A8 3A A6 74 18 
    0A 96 4A 2D A8 FA 26 59 E4 22 FD EE 71 AE 63 6B 
$ 
$ ./dgst_test sha512 what\ the\ cat\ dragged\ in
Input is 23 bytes : "what the cat dragged in"
    77 68 61 74 20 74 68 65 20 63 61 74 20 64 72 61 
    67 67 65 64 20 69 6E 

Message digest algorithm sha512 :
    0D 50 57 C5 B1 0D D8 A9 DF B6 1B BA 14 95 A0 33 
    EB 5E 23 C7 65 86 69 3A B2 99 EC 47 30 2F 2F 9B 
    B2 44 F7 B1 3C 54 79 7B 4F 86 60 B3 7D F5 6B 56 
    9D A7 A9 8B B8 B9 A1 AD B3 36 66 4E 69 38 9E EE 
$ 
$ ./dgst_test sha3-512 what\ the\ cat\ dragged\ in
Input is 23 bytes : "what the cat dragged in"
    77 68 61 74 20 74 68 65 20 63 61 74 20 64 72 61 
    67 67 65 64 20 69 6E 

Message digest algorithm sha3-512 :
    4D DB 77 B0 43 10 94 3A B1 50 EB 87 FD C2 C9 90 
    BD 25 9E 59 EB 38 B1 A4 C0 68 AE 83 8E E5 91 F0 
    8A 83 34 65 B9 9E 98 35 1B 4C 5E 3B 41 E4 84 D0 
    C3 6E D5 0C 42 95 E0 6F AC 57 CD F3 3E FB 79 36 
$ 


